1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,480
Imagine a single teaspoon
of matter from a neutron star.

2
00:00:04,107 --> 00:00:06,347
On Earth, it would weigh
billions of tonnes.

3
00:00:07,381 --> 00:00:10,101
That's several times the mass
of every human being alive.

4
00:00:10,331 --> 00:00:12,041
In one teaspoon.

5
00:00:12,611 --> 00:00:14,491
Which raises two questions.

6
00:00:15,171 --> 00:00:18,191
How can that much mass
fit into something so small?

7
00:00:18,631 --> 00:00:21,551
And if you could somehow bring it here,
what would it do?

8
00:00:23,891 --> 00:00:28,691
Start with something ordinary.
A rock. A piece of iron. Your hand.

9
00:00:29,345 --> 00:00:30,825
It all feels solid.

10
00:00:31,005 --> 00:00:34,305
But zoom in, and keep zooming,
until you reach the atoms.

11
00:00:35,117 --> 00:00:39,097
An atom has a tiny nucleus at its centre,
with electrons around it.

12
00:00:39,177 --> 00:00:40,317
And in between...

13
00:00:40,547 --> 00:00:42,157
almost nothing.

14
00:00:42,546 --> 00:00:45,326
If an atom were the size
of a football stadium,

15
00:00:45,526 --> 00:00:48,226
the nucleus would be a pea
in the middle of the pitch.

16
00:00:48,586 --> 00:00:49,566
And yet that pea holds

17
00:00:49,796 --> 00:00:53,266
more than ninety-nine point nine per cent
of the atom's mass.

18
00:00:53,909 --> 00:00:56,869
So ordinary matter is mostly empty space.

19
00:00:57,709 --> 00:01:01,089
If you could squeeze the emptiness
out of every person on Earth,

20
00:01:01,169 --> 00:01:03,749
all of humanity would fit
into a sugar cube.

21
00:01:04,332 --> 00:01:05,732
That's the first clue.

22
00:01:06,532 --> 00:01:09,692
A neutron star is what happens
when matter is forced into a state

23
00:01:09,922 --> 00:01:12,672
where ordinary atoms can no longer exist.

24
00:01:13,411 --> 00:01:17,051
To see how that's possible,
we need to look inside the nucleus.

25
00:01:18,123 --> 00:01:20,903
A nucleus is made of protons and neutrons.

26
00:01:20,983 --> 00:01:23,823
And protons are positively charged.

27
00:01:24,579 --> 00:01:27,239
Two positive charges repel each other.

28
00:01:27,799 --> 00:01:31,619
Pack several protons together,
and they should push themselves apart.

29
00:01:32,178 --> 00:01:37,758
And yet nuclei exist. Carbon exists.
Oxygen exists. You exist.

30
00:01:38,507 --> 00:01:40,387
Something holds them together.

31
00:01:40,967 --> 00:01:44,427
It's the strong interaction
between protons and neutrons.

32
00:01:44,627 --> 00:01:48,907
At very short distances,
it overpowers the electric repulsion.

33
00:01:49,394 --> 00:01:51,134
But its reach is tiny.

34
00:01:51,294 --> 00:01:55,134
Beyond the width of a few protons,
it fades away very quickly.

35
00:01:55,692 --> 00:01:59,492
So particles have to get extremely close
before it can grab them.

36
00:02:00,012 --> 00:02:01,632
Which brings us to the squeezing.

37
00:02:04,152 --> 00:02:07,552
A star many times heavier than the Sun
spends millions of years

38
00:02:07,782 --> 00:02:10,632
fusing light elements into heavier ones.

39
00:02:11,309 --> 00:02:13,989
It ends up with a core of iron,
heavier than the Sun

40
00:02:14,219 --> 00:02:16,269
and roughly the size of the Earth.

41
00:02:16,931 --> 00:02:18,471
Iron is a dead end.

42
00:02:18,551 --> 00:02:21,291
Fusing it doesn't release energy.

43
00:02:22,111 --> 00:02:23,531
So once the core gets heavy enough,

44
00:02:23,761 --> 00:02:26,871
it can no longer hold itself up
against its own gravity.

45
00:02:27,676 --> 00:02:28,876
It collapses.

46
00:02:29,176 --> 00:02:33,976
In less than a second, something the size
of a planet shrinks to the size of a city.

47
00:02:34,665 --> 00:02:38,025
Under that pressure,
protons start capturing electrons.

48
00:02:39,065 --> 00:02:41,105
Each capture turns
a proton into a neutron,

49
00:02:41,335 --> 00:02:44,965
and releases a ghostly particle
called a neutrino.

50
00:02:45,493 --> 00:02:48,093
A flood of neutrinos
bursts out of the core,

51
00:02:48,173 --> 00:02:50,073
carrying away most of the energy,

52
00:02:50,413 --> 00:02:54,093
and helping to blast the outer layers
into space in a supernova.

53
00:02:55,384 --> 00:02:58,784
What's left behind
is no longer made of ordinary atoms.

54
00:02:59,664 --> 00:03:03,064
The electrons that kept the nuclei apart
are mostly gone.

55
00:03:03,674 --> 00:03:06,514
It's typically about
twenty-five kilometres across,

56
00:03:06,594 --> 00:03:08,534
made mostly of neutrons,

57
00:03:08,614 --> 00:03:11,674
packed to roughly the density
of an atomic nucleus.

58
00:03:12,410 --> 00:03:15,650
It's tempting to call it
one giant atomic nucleus.

59
00:03:15,750 --> 00:03:17,530
But it isn't.

60
00:03:17,876 --> 00:03:21,616
The crust is a lattice
of strange, neutron-heavy nuclei.

61
00:03:22,156 --> 00:03:26,096
Deeper down, they merge into shapes
physicists call nuclear pasta.

62
00:03:26,891 --> 00:03:30,871
Below that is a fluid of neutrons.
And at the very centre...

63
00:03:30,991 --> 00:03:32,391
nobody knows yet.

64
00:03:33,027 --> 00:03:36,227
What holds it up is the pressure
of this ultra-dense matter.

65
00:03:36,967 --> 00:03:39,867
Partly a quantum effect,
and partly the strong interaction

66
00:03:40,097 --> 00:03:42,007
pushing back at close range.

67
00:03:42,503 --> 00:03:46,163
But beyond a certain mass,
no known pressure can stop the collapse.

68
00:03:46,963 --> 00:03:48,823
The star becomes a black hole.

69
00:03:49,892 --> 00:03:51,752
So that's how the mass fits.

70
00:03:52,492 --> 00:03:56,452
Gravity crushes matter into a state
where ordinary atoms can't exist,

71
00:03:56,532 --> 00:03:58,652
and packs it as tightly as a nucleus.

72
00:04:01,532 --> 00:04:03,372
Now for the second question.

73
00:04:03,992 --> 00:04:07,132
Suppose you could scoop out a teaspoon
and bring it to Earth.

74
00:04:07,859 --> 00:04:09,059
There's a catch.

75
00:04:09,439 --> 00:04:12,519
The strong interaction
isn't what holds this matter together.

76
00:04:13,259 --> 00:04:15,559
On their own, neutrons don't stick.

77
00:04:16,139 --> 00:04:18,959
It's the gravity of the whole star
that keeps it squeezed.

78
00:04:19,823 --> 00:04:22,783
Take it out of the star,
and nothing would hold it in.

79
00:04:23,323 --> 00:04:26,963
Its own pressure would take over,
and it would decompress violently.

80
00:04:27,835 --> 00:04:31,815
As the matter decompressed, it would have
to turn into ordinary matter.

81
00:04:32,595 --> 00:04:35,655
Even the energy difference
between a neutron and a hydrogen atom

82
00:04:35,885 --> 00:04:40,545
adds up to more than the asteroid
that wiped out the dinosaurs.

83
00:04:43,045 --> 00:04:45,245
And this isn't just a thought experiment.

84
00:04:46,005 --> 00:04:49,025
In 2017,
we watched something like it happen.

85
00:04:49,429 --> 00:04:53,609
Two neutron stars, about a hundred
and thirty million light-years away,

86
00:04:53,749 --> 00:04:55,929
spiralled into each other and collided.

87
00:04:56,693 --> 00:04:59,573
Gravitational-wave detectors
felt the collision.

88
00:05:00,353 --> 00:05:03,213
Then telescopes all over the world
turned to look.

89
00:05:03,788 --> 00:05:08,888
They saw debris from the neutron stars
expanding into space, glowing as it went.

90
00:05:09,580 --> 00:05:13,580
As that neutron-rich matter decompressed,
it built heavy atoms.

91
00:05:14,660 --> 00:05:17,540
Astronomers identified what
is most likely strontium,

92
00:05:17,770 --> 00:05:20,740
and models suggest gold and platinum too.

93
00:05:21,347 --> 00:05:24,207
Some of the gold on Earth may have
been made in collisions like this.

94
00:05:24,437 --> 00:05:28,127
Neutron-star matter,
released from the pressure.

95
00:05:30,737 --> 00:05:34,117
But there's an even stranger question
hiding in that teaspoon.

96
00:05:34,665 --> 00:05:36,165
Not why it's so dense.

97
00:05:36,245 --> 00:05:38,545
Why it weighs anything at all.

98
00:05:40,335 --> 00:05:45,575
Every neutron and every proton is made
of three smaller particles, called quarks.

99
00:05:45,991 --> 00:05:49,571
So you'd expect a proton
to weigh as much as its three quarks.

100
00:05:49,931 --> 00:05:51,351
Let's add them up.

101
00:05:51,935 --> 00:05:53,155
About one per cent.

102
00:05:53,385 --> 00:05:57,215
The quarks account for
roughly one per cent of the proton.

103
00:05:58,457 --> 00:06:01,417
The other ninety-nine per cent
comes from energy.

104
00:06:02,179 --> 00:06:05,439
The energy of quarks moving
at nearly the speed of light,

105
00:06:05,619 --> 00:06:08,139
and of the gluon fields
binding them together.

106
00:06:08,803 --> 00:06:11,123
Einstein showed that energy has mass.

107
00:06:11,203 --> 00:06:13,723
E equals m c squared.

108
00:06:14,103 --> 00:06:17,843
Inside a proton, that's where almost
all the mass comes from.

109
00:06:18,354 --> 00:06:21,654
You may have heard that the Higgs
field gives particles their mass.

110
00:06:22,014 --> 00:06:24,174
It does give quarks theirs.

111
00:06:24,934 --> 00:06:28,694
But the quarks' own mass
is only about one per cent of the proton.

112
00:06:29,534 --> 00:06:31,674
So the matter in your body
is mostly massive

113
00:06:31,904 --> 00:06:35,294
because of the energy of the fields
that bind it together.

114
00:06:37,624 --> 00:06:39,464
And the same goes for the teaspoon.

115
00:06:40,364 --> 00:06:44,724
Billions of tonnes, and almost all of it
is the energy of the strong interaction.

116
00:06:45,509 --> 00:06:49,629
Using supercomputers,
physicists can calculate the proton's mass

117
00:06:49,859 --> 00:06:54,309
from quantum chromodynamics,
and the result agrees with experiment.

118
00:06:54,970 --> 00:06:59,270
But that calculation starts from a few
numbers the theory itself doesn't predict.

119
00:06:59,815 --> 00:07:01,975
How strong the strong interaction is.

120
00:07:02,575 --> 00:07:04,295
How heavy the quarks are.

121
00:07:04,615 --> 00:07:07,375
Those have to be measured,
and then put in by hand.

122
00:07:08,021 --> 00:07:12,301
The Standard Model of particle physics
contains around twenty such numbers.

123
00:07:12,981 --> 00:07:16,441
If the universe had a settings menu,
these would be the sliders.

124
00:07:18,901 --> 00:07:28,421
Take the speed of light.
Exactly 299,792,458 metres per second.

125
00:07:29,161 --> 00:07:30,781
That number looks fundamental.

126
00:07:31,431 --> 00:07:34,511
But its exact value
depends on the units we choose.

127
00:07:35,391 --> 00:07:39,771
Since 1983, the metre has been defined
using the speed of light.

128
00:07:40,446 --> 00:07:44,906
Measure in feet, or in some alien unit,
and the number changes completely.

129
00:07:45,826 --> 00:07:47,886
The speed itself matters enormously.

130
00:07:48,726 --> 00:07:50,766
The number is a fact about our ruler.

131
00:07:51,826 --> 00:07:54,146
But some numbers have no units at all.

132
00:07:54,226 --> 00:07:56,066
And those are different.

133
00:07:56,788 --> 00:07:59,728
The most famous one
is the fine-structure constant.

134
00:08:00,748 --> 00:08:04,248
It sets how strongly electric charges
interact with light.

135
00:08:04,929 --> 00:08:08,829
It helps set how big atoms are,
all that empty space from the beginning,

136
00:08:09,059 --> 00:08:11,089
and the strength of chemical bonds.

137
00:08:11,851 --> 00:08:13,704
Its value is about 1/137.

138
00:08:13,934 --> 00:08:19,651
Metres, feet or alien units,
everyone gets the same number.

139
00:08:20,203 --> 00:08:22,743
We can measure it
with extraordinary precision.

140
00:08:22,903 --> 00:08:26,523
But we still don't know
why its value is what it is.

141
00:08:29,003 --> 00:08:32,023
Some calculations suggest
that changing certain constants

142
00:08:32,253 --> 00:08:36,983
could leave a universe with no stable
stars, or no complex chemistry.

143
00:08:37,697 --> 00:08:38,517
Is that luck?

144
00:08:38,597 --> 00:08:40,917
Is there a deeper theory that fixes them?

145
00:08:41,397 --> 00:08:43,597
Or are we one universe among many?

146
00:08:43,937 --> 00:08:45,137
Nobody knows.

147
00:08:47,739 --> 00:08:49,699
Think about that teaspoon again.

148
00:08:50,179 --> 00:08:52,419
Billions of tonnes of crushed matter.

149
00:08:53,170 --> 00:08:56,030
To explain it,
we went from atoms to nuclei,

150
00:08:56,110 --> 00:08:59,670
from nuclei to neutrons,
from neutrons to quarks and fields.

151
00:09:00,255 --> 00:09:03,375
And at the bottom of all those layers,
we found a handful of numbers

152
00:09:03,605 --> 00:09:06,075
our best theories simply take as given.

153
00:09:08,225 --> 00:09:12,145
Even if a deeper theory explains them
one day, we'll have a new question.

154
00:09:12,905 --> 00:09:14,105
Why that theory?

155
00:09:15,345 --> 00:09:17,705
Maybe the universe has no settings menu.

156
00:09:17,785 --> 00:09:20,085
Maybe it does. Or maybe...

157
00:09:20,165 --> 00:09:21,965
we just haven't found it yet.

158
00:09:23,855 --> 00:09:25,715
So here's a question for you.

159
00:09:26,455 --> 00:09:31,075
If physicists one day derive 1/137
from something deeper,

160
00:09:31,315 --> 00:09:34,755
would that answer the question,
or just move it one level down?

161
00:09:35,255 --> 00:09:36,925
Let me know in the comments.
